= 1
{parent=2007/A}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=1}

Differentiate the <Lagrangian> with respect to the generalized velocities:
$$
\boxed{p_j=\frac{\partial L}{\partial\dot x_j}=m\dot x_j+eA_j.}
$$
The <Legendre transform> cancels the vector-<potential energy> term, giving $H=p\cdot\dot x-L=m|\dot x|^2/2+V$. Eliminating <velocity> with $m\dot x=p-eA$ therefore yields
$$
\boxed{H=\frac{|p-eA|^2}{2m}+V.}
$$
For the given gauge, set $\pi_1=p_1+eBx_2/2$, $\pi_2=p_2-eBx_1/2$, $\pi_3=p_3$. <Hamilton's equations> give $\dot x_j=\pi_j/m$, $\dot p_1=(eB/2)\dot x_2$, $\dot p_2=-(eB/2)\dot x_1$, $\dot p_3=0$. Hence the conserved quantities are
$$
\boxed{p_3,\qquad K_1=p_1-\frac{eB}{2}x_2,\qquad K_2=p_2+\frac{eB}{2}x_1,\qquad H.}
$$
The two transverse combinations are components of <magnetic pseudomomentum>. **The printed conserved combinations omit the <electric charge> factor $e$.** With the stated <vector potential> and general <electric charge> they need the factors displayed above; as printed they hold only if $e=1$ or if <electric charge> is already absorbed into the field convention.

The initial data give $K_1=K_2=p_3=0$. Thus $p_1=eBx_2/2$, $p_2=-eBx_1/2$ throughout, and
$$
\dot x_1=\omega_cx_2,\qquad\dot x_2=-\omega_cx_1,\qquad\omega_c=eB/m.
$$
Solving with the initial point gives
$$
\boxed{x_1=a\sin(\omega_ct),\quad x_2=a\cos(\omega_ct),\quad x_3=0.}
$$
Consequently $x_1^2+x_2^2=a^2$: the <guiding centre> is the origin and the signed <cyclotron frequency> fixes the rotation direction. The <energy> is the constant $e^2B^2a^2/(2m)$.

= 2
{parent=2007/A}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2}

Inside the box, the <time-independent Schrödinger equation> is $-\hbar^2\Psi''/(2m)=E\Psi$. The zero endpoint values eliminate the cosine term and require $ka=n\pi$. There is no nonzero zero-<energy> solution with both endpoints zero. <Normalization> uses $\int_0^a\sin^2(n\pi x/a)\,dx=a/2$, so
$$
\boxed{E_n=\frac{n^2\pi^2\hbar^2}{2ma^2},\qquad
\Psi_n(x)=\sqrt{\frac2a}\sin\frac{n\pi x}{a},\qquad n=1,2,\ldots.}
$$
Insert the expansion into $i\hbar\partial_t\psi=H\psi$ and take its <inner product> with $\Psi_m$. <Orthonormality> gives $i\hbar\dot c_m=E_mc_m$ for each $m$, and conversely these equations make every expansion coefficient of the residual zero. Thus $c_n(t)=c_n(0)e^{-iE_nt/\hbar}$ in the usual Hilbert-space or strong-solution class appropriate to the expansion.

For the specified state,
$$
\boxed{\psi(t,x)=\frac35e^{-iE_3t/\hbar}\Psi_3(x)+\frac45e^{-iE_4t/\hbar}\Psi_4(x).}
$$
The modes are real, so the <interference> term is a cosine of the <energy> difference. Writing $s_n=\sin(n\pi x/a)$,
$$
\boxed{|\psi(t,x)|^2=\frac2{25a}\left[9s_3^2+16s_4^2+24s_3s_4\cos\!\left(\frac{7\pi^2\hbar t}{2ma^2}\right)\right].}
$$
Its integral remains one because the cross term integrates to zero. The probabilities of the two <energy> outcomes are fixed at $9/25$ and $16/25$, even though the position <probability density> oscillates.

= 3
{parent=2007/A}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=3}

For a decaying <bound state> take $\alpha>0$. Substituting $F=fe^{-\alpha r}$ gives $F''=e^{-\alpha r}(f''-2\alpha f'+\alpha^2f)$. The term involving the <energy> cancels $\alpha^2f$, leaving the displayed equation with
$$
\boxed{A=\frac{2mK}{\hbar^2}.}
$$
At the lowest power of a series from the <Frobenius method> $f=\sum_{k\geq0}a_kr^{k+c}$, the <indicial equation> is $c(c-1)-\ell(\ell+1)=0$. Its roots are $\ell+1$ and $-\ell$. The latter makes $F\sim r^{-\ell}$ and the <radial probability density> integral $\int|F|^2dr$ diverge for positive integral $\ell$. Hence $\boxed{c=\ell+1}$.

Equating the remaining coefficients gives
$$
\boxed{a_{k+1}=\frac{2\alpha(k+\ell+1)-A}{(k+1)(k+2\ell+2)}a_k.}
$$
Unless the numerator vanishes, the regular confluent-hypergeometric series contains a large-radius growing branch with the exponential factor $e^{2\alpha r}$. Multiplication by the external $e^{-\alpha r}$ then leaves exponential growth, so it cannot be a <normalizable> <bound state>. Equivalently, the recurrence at large $k$ has $a_{k+1}/a_k\sim2\alpha/k$, indicating that growing exponential scale; regularity at the origin has already fixed the combination of solutions, leaving termination as the way to remove it.

If the polynomial ends at degree index $N\geq0$, its last recurrence numerator gives $A=2\alpha(N+\ell+1)$. With $n=N+\ell+1>\ell$,
$$
\boxed{\alpha=\frac A{2n},\qquad E_n=-\frac{\hbar^2A^2}{8mn^2}=-\frac{mK^2}{2\hbar^2n^2}.}
$$
For the separate angle-independent exponential, <normalization> includes the full <solid angle>:
$$
1=4\pi|N|^2\int_0^\infty r^2e^{-2\alpha r}\,dr
=4\pi|N|^2\frac{2}{(2\alpha)^3}=\frac{\pi|N|^2}{\alpha^3}.
$$
Thus one can choose $\boxed{N=\sqrt{\alpha^3/\pi}}$, up to an overall <phase>. Angle independence corresponds to the $\ell=0$ extension, distinct from the positive-$\ell$ family used in the indicial discussion.

= 4
{parent=2007/A}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=4}

A <pure state> is a unit vector in a complex <Hilbert space>, with vectors differing by a <global phase> representing the same physical state. An <observable> is a <self-adjoint operator>, with <measurement> probabilities determined by its <spectral projections>. For vectors in the relevant <operator domains>,
$$
\boxed{E_\psi[A]=\langle\psi,A\psi\rangle,\qquad
\Delta_\psi[A]^2=\langle\psi,A^2\psi\rangle-E_\psi[A]^2
=\|(A-E_\psi[A])\psi\|^2.}
$$
A squared norm vanishes exactly when its vector vanishes. Therefore zero <dispersion> is equivalent to $A\psi=E_\psi[A]\psi$.

In the position representation, $(X\psi)(x)=x\psi(x)$ and $(P\psi)(x)=-i\hbar\psi'(x)$. On a common differentiable invariant domain,
$$
(XP-PX)\psi=-i\hbar x\psi'+i\hbar(x\psi)'=i\hbar\psi,
$$
so $\boxed{[X,P]=i\hbar I}$.

For two <observables> set $a=\|A\psi\|^2$, $b=\|B\psi\|^2$, $c=E_\psi[i[A,B]]$. For real $t$,
$$
0\leq\|(A-itB)\psi\|^2=a-ct+bt^2.
$$
If $b>0$, nonpositivity of its discriminant yields $\boxed{4ab\geq c^2}$. Equality holds exactly when the minimum squared norm vanishes, namely
$$
\boxed{A\psi=itB\psi\quad\text{for some real }t,\quad t=c/(2b).}
$$
The degenerate case must also be included: if $B\psi=0$, then $b=c=0$ and equality holds for every $A\psi$. Thus the complete criterion is that $B\psi=0$, or that the displayed real-$t$ relation holds. This includes $A\psi=0$ by choosing $t=0$ when $b>0$.

Apply the inequality to centred operators $A=P-E_\psi[P]I$, $B=X-E_\psi[X]I$. Their squared norms are the <variances> and their <commutator> is $[P,X]=-i\hbar I$, giving
$$
\boxed{\Delta_\psi[P]^2\Delta_\psi[X]^2\geq\frac{\hbar^2}{4}.}
$$
An <eigenvector> of either operator would have its corresponding <dispersion> zero, contradicting this positive lower bound if the other <dispersion> were finite and the stated domain conditions held. In the usual $L^2(\mathbb R)$ representation there are in fact no <normalizable> exact position or momentum <eigenvectors>; their generalized <eigenvectors> are not counterexamples. The equality condition above is the <uncentered second-moment commutator inequality>.

